## Platform baseline

| Technology | Responsibility | Engineering implication |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| Kotlin | Application language and explicit data contracts | Prefer immutable values; model nullability and failures deliberately. |
| Jetpack Compose | Declarative UI | Render state, emit callbacks, and keep composition free of uncontrolled side effects. |
| ViewModel + StateFlow | Screen state and coordination | Expose read-only state; keep writable flows private. |
| Coroutines | Structured asynchronous work | Choose an owner, propagate cancellation, and make data operations safe to call from the main thread. |
| Room | Structured local persistence | Use account-scoped keys, suspend DAO operations, and tested schema migrations. |
| Retrofit / approved HTTP SDK | Remote API integration | Inject authenticated networking, validate responses, and map HTTP failures explicitly. |
| Hilt | Dependency construction and scope | Scope objects according to ownership; dependency injection does not manage authentication automatically. |

Use the versions pinned in the approved project template and version catalog. Kotlin, the Compose compiler/plugin, Android Gradle Plugin, Gradle, JDK, Android SDK, and libraries must form a compatible set. A JDK build setting is separate from Android Runtime, which executes the application on devices.

**Example API requirements:** Room with `@Upsert` support, lifecycle-aware Compose collection, Kotlin serialization configured as Retrofit's converter, Material 3, and Hilt integration for navigation-scoped ViewModels. Resolve the Hilt Compose import against the version approved in your application; it has changed between library generations.

**Internal policy versus Android guidance:** This platform uses use cases between ViewModels and repositories. Android's general guidance treats the domain layer as optional depending on complexity. The stricter separation here is a platform convention, not a universal Android requirement. See [Android architecture recommendations](https://developer.android.com/topic/architecture/recommendations).

### State ownership

| State or operation | Owner |
| :--- | :--- |
| Temporary component interaction, such as an expanded section | Local Compose state |
| Saveable UI-only values, such as an input draft | Appropriate `rememberSaveable` or saved-state integration |
| Rendered profile, progress, and safe error category | ViewModel StateFlow |
| Cached profile retained across process restarts | Room repository boundary |
| Screen-visible loading in this example | The route's lifecycle-controlled coroutine |
| Work that must survive navigation | A deliberately longer-lived owner; use WorkManager for suitable persistent background work |
| Credentials and token lifecycle | Approved authentication/secure-storage SDK |

Loading and content can coexist: retain a cached profile while refreshing it. A transient network failure should not replace useful content with an empty error screen.

## Architecture boundaries

| Layer | Owns | Must not depend on |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| Presentation | Compose views, screen state, ViewModel | Retrofit endpoints, Room DAOs, data implementations |
| Domain | Profile values, validation, repository protocol | Compose, Android Context, Room, Retrofit |
| Data | DTOs, entities, mappers, repository implementation | Presentation code |
| Application infrastructure / DI | Authenticated clients, session guard, dispatchers, clock, object scopes | Product rendering decisions |

The domain example uses constructor-injection annotations. “Independent of Android” means no Android framework dependency; it does not mean no library dependencies.

