## Application integration

1. **Confirm the supported toolchain.** Use project-managed Kotlin, Compose, lifecycle, Room, Hilt, Retrofit, and serialization versions. Configure their compiler/code-generation requirements in the application.
2. **Register the schema safely.** Add the account-scoped entity and DAO to the real Room database. Migrate old single-user-key records deliberately; do not accidentally expose them under a different account.
3. **Bind approved infrastructure.** Supply authenticated networking, the active-account guard, diagnostics, and existing dispatcher/clock abstractions. Ensure every HTTP request captures the intended session.
4. **Scope navigation correctly.** Supply the trusted account identity and validated route ID. Own the ViewModel at the appropriate navigation destination and remove protected back-stack entries on account/session changes.
5. **Define cache policy.** Decide which personal data may be stored, how long it remains valid, and which protection/backup settings apply. Room does not encrypt a database automatically.
6. **Implement session recovery.** Block protected access, cancel old work, clear protected records and credentials, and replace session-owned dependencies. Deleting one profile is not full logout or revocation handling.
7. **Apply product UX.** Use enterprise theme tokens, localized strings, accessible progress/error states, and real sign-in or permission-recovery actions.
8. **Verify in the application.** Build the actual target, run migrations and coroutine tests, and exercise supported devices and Android versions.

Use the approved secure-storage abstraction for credentials. Android Keystore manages cryptographic keys; it is not a general-purpose token database. Hardware-backed protection depends on device and key configuration. Keep credentials out of source, UI state, logs, and profile entities.

This read/refresh example does not implement a durable outbox, background sync, circuit breaking, or automatic retry loops. Add those through approved shared capabilities when the feature requires them. Choose retry rules according to idempotency and error category, not simply “retry every failure.”

