# Cross-Platform React Native Standards & Architecture

> **Confidential — internal use only.** Keep code, configuration, and operational evidence within approved systems. Use synthetic examples; never expose credentials, sensitive Redux state, private endpoints, or personal data through logs, screenshots, debugging tools, or AI prompts.

Build features with clear boundaries: **views render state, Redux Toolkit coordinates updates, use cases apply domain rules, and repositories own data access**.

**React Native is our strongly preferred choice for new mobile applications.** Use TypeScript and Redux Toolkit as established by the team.

This page defines the React Native reference architecture and demonstrates a user-profile feature. The example displays validated cached content, requests fresh data, saves a valid response, and updates the screen without allowing cancelled or older requests to overwrite newer state.

<Aside type="tip" title="Team standard · Redux Toolkit">
Use Redux Toolkit for shared application state, React Redux for typed component subscriptions, and the application's established Redux data-fetching pattern. Keep transport and storage behind repository contracts. Components and reducers must not make HTTP calls or access MMKV directly.
</Aside>

## Start here

