## Push Notifications & Validated Routing

**Responsibility:** Manage registration and token changes, adapt OS callbacks, and convert untrusted notification data into authorized in-app navigation.

Push delivery is not a reliable transaction or an authorization signal. The app must recover authoritative state when it starts, resumes, or reconnects even when no push arrived.

<MermaidZoom title="Push routing · validate first, authorize before navigation">

```mermaid
sequenceDiagram
    autonumber
    participant G as Enterprise gateway
    participant OS as APNs / FCM and device OS
    participant A as Platform push adapter
    participant R as Application router
    participant S as Session and API
    participant UI as Feature screen

    G->>OS: Notification or background update
    alt OS permits delivery
        OS->>A: Supported callback / initial launch payload
        A->>A: Extract bounded payload, validate schema
        A->>R: Typed intent with event identity
        R->>R: Deduplicate and wait for app readiness
        R->>S: Resolve session and resource authorization
        alt Access allowed
            S-->>R: Authorized destination
            R->>UI: Navigate with validated arguments
        else Access unavailable
            S-->>R: Sign-in required / access denied / missing resource
            R->>UI: Show supported recovery route
        end
    else Delivery delayed, dropped, or unavailable
        Note over S,UI: Reconcile state through normal startup/resume flows
    end
```

</MermaidZoom>

The OS may present a notification without invoking the application's foreground callback. iOS notification service extensions are a separate, conditional path for eligible notification modification, not a universal Android/iOS delivery stage. They have time limits and need a fallback that preserves completion behavior.

### Platform integration checklist

| Android | iOS | React Native |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| Configure channels and notification runtime permission where required. | Configure capabilities, APNs registration, notification authorization, and delegates. | Complete both native setups and use the installed SDK's supported API style. |
| Separate notification/data payload behavior and foreground/background handling. | Treat foreground presentation, notification taps, cold launch, and background updates separately. | Handle foreground events, opened-from-background events, initial notification, and token refresh. |
| Use explicit app navigation intents; make pending intents immutable where supported and give destinations correct identity. | Route through the app coordinator; do not send arbitrary payload URLs to `UIApplication.open`. | Register background handlers at the SDK-required entry point; ordinary component effects are insufficient for that path. |
| Keep callback work bounded; delegate suitable longer work to an approved scheduler. | Call completion handlers on every path and respect background execution limits. | Retain unsubscribe functions and remove subscriptions with their owner. |

Android 13+ notification permission affects posting notifications; account for target/OS behavior in the actual app. FCM notification messages can be displayed by the OS while backgrounded. Apple's background notification delivery is not guaranteed. See [Android permissions](https://developer.android.com/develop/ui/compose/notifications/notification-permission), [FCM Android delivery](https://firebase.google.com/docs/cloud-messaging/android/receive-messages), and [Apple background updates](https://developer.apple.com/documentation/usernotifications/pushing-background-updates-to-your-app).

### Typed payload boundary · React Native example

This sample uses a structured intent instead of opening a supplied URI. The payload is a navigation hint; the server remains authoritative about resource access.

`src/core/push/pushIntent.ts`

```typescript
import { z } from 'zod';

const destinationSchema = z.discriminatedUnion('kind', [
  z.object({ kind: z.literal('home') }),
  z.object({
    kind: z.literal('order'),
    orderId: z.string().regex(/^[A-Za-z0-9_-]{1,128}$/),
  }),
]);

const pushIntentSchema = z.object({
  version: z.literal(1),
  eventId: z.string().regex(/^[A-Za-z0-9_-]{1,128}$/),
  expiresAtMillis: z.number().int().nonnegative(),
  destination: destinationSchema,
});
export type PushIntent = z.infer<typeof pushIntentSchema>;

export function parsePushIntent(raw: unknown, nowMillis: number): PushIntent | null {
  if (typeof raw !== 'string' || raw.length > 2048) return null;
  let decoded: unknown;
  try { decoded = JSON.parse(raw); }
  catch { return null; } // Invalid external input is an expected rejection path.
  const parsed = pushIntentSchema.safeParse(decoded);
  if (!parsed.success || parsed.data.expiresAtMillis <= nowMillis) return null;
  return parsed.data;
}
```

The application limit above is a string-length bound, not the provider's byte-size limit. Set actual payload limits in the gateway/adapter as well. Expiry checks are client freshness checks and depend on the clock; they do not authenticate a command. Rooted/compromised clients and server authorization require separate controls.

### Subscription ownership · adapter contract

`src/core/push/attachPushListeners.ts`

```typescript
import type { PushIntent } from './pushIntent';

type Unsubscribe = () => void;
export interface PushAdapter {
  onForeground(handler: (intent: PushIntent) => void): Unsubscribe;
  onOpened(handler: (intent: PushIntent) => void): Unsubscribe;
  initialIntent(): Promise<PushIntent | null>;
}

export function attachPushListeners(
  adapter: PushAdapter,
  receiveForeground: (intent: PushIntent) => Promise<void>,
  routeIntent: (intent: PushIntent) => Promise<void>,
  reportFailure: (category: 'foreground' | 'initial-notification' | 'routing') => void,
): Unsubscribe {
  let active = true;
  const deliver = (intent: PushIntent): void => {
    if (!active) return;
    void Promise.resolve().then(() => active ? routeIntent(intent) : undefined).catch(() => {
      if (active) reportFailure('routing');
    });
  };
  const removeForeground = adapter.onForeground((intent) => {
    if (!active) return;
    void Promise.resolve().then(() => active ? receiveForeground(intent) : undefined).catch(() => {
      if (active) reportFailure('foreground');
    });
  });
  let removeOpened: Unsubscribe;
  try {
    removeOpened = adapter.onOpened(deliver);
  } catch (error: unknown) {
    active = false;
    removeForeground();
    throw error;
  }
  void Promise.resolve().then(() => adapter.initialIntent()).then((intent) => {
    if (intent) deliver(intent);
  }).catch(() => {
    if (active) reportFailure('initial-notification');
  });
  return () => {
    active = false;
    try { removeForeground(); }
    finally { removeOpened(); }
  };
}
```

The adapter emits validated intents; map the SDK's actual payload shape before this boundary. Diagnostics must be non-throwing and contain no payload/token data. Subscription removal is synchronous, non-throwing, and must be safe when cleanup repeats. The initial-notification call is wrapped in a promise chain so a synchronous adapter failure is also reported.

<Aside type="tip" title="Hint · one router handles every entry point">
Cold-start notification, background tap, explicit foreground-banner interaction, and ordinary deep links must converge on the same application router. That router serializes routing, deduplicates event IDs, checks the current session and backend authorization, and constructs a valid back stack. Foreground receipt updates/presents content but does not auto-navigate. Listener cleanup prevents new delivery; it does not cancel a navigation already in progress. The router must recheck session identity before committing navigation.
</Aside>

For URL-based deep links, validate scheme, host, path, parameters, and supported destinations; prefer verified app/universal links where applicable. Do not pass arbitrary incoming URLs directly to the OS. A regex-valid resource ID is not proof that the current account may view it.

Never log registration tokens or complete notification payloads. Synchronize token registration/rotation to the backend, handle account association changes, and distinguish provider acceptance, device delivery, display, and user-open metrics.


### Android · payload validation and FCM ingestion

`core/push/PushIntent.kt`

```kotlin
package com.enterprise.mobile.core.push

import kotlinx.serialization.SerialName
import kotlinx.serialization.Serializable
import kotlinx.serialization.json.Json

@Serializable
sealed interface PushDestination {
    @Serializable @SerialName("home")
    data object Home : PushDestination
    @Serializable @SerialName("order")
    data class Order(val orderId: String) : PushDestination
}

@Serializable
data class PushIntent(
    val version: Int,
    val eventId: String,
    val expiresAtMillis: Long,
    val destination: PushDestination,
)

object PushIntentDecoder {
    private val json = Json { classDiscriminator = "kind"; ignoreUnknownKeys = true }
    private val idPattern = Regex("[A-Za-z0-9_-]{1,128}")

    fun parse(raw: String?, nowMillis: Long): PushIntent? {
        if (raw == null || raw.length > 2048) return null
        val intent = try { json.decodeFromString<PushIntent>(raw) }
        catch (_: kotlinx.serialization.SerializationException) { return null }
        catch (_: IllegalArgumentException) { return null }
        if (intent.version != 1 || !idPattern.matches(intent.eventId) ||
            intent.expiresAtMillis <= nowMillis) return null
        val destination = intent.destination
        if (destination is PushDestination.Order && !idPattern.matches(destination.orderId)) return null
        return intent
    }
}
```

