## Test the foundations before rollout

The required target is **100% within the defined production scope and metrics**, with every production file accounted for. Account for generated assets/code and third-party dependencies explicitly; do not silently exclude failure paths. Unit coverage does not replace native integration, accessibility, or real-device checks.

| Foundation | Fast automated checks | Native/device verification |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| Design | Token schema, unresolved aliases, paired light/dark values, preference resolution. | Actual component contrast, text sizing, system theme changes, and every supported brand. |
| Security | Scope construction, result mapping, missing-item and failure behavior. | Device lock, key invalidation, biometric behavior where required, backup/restore policy, and deletion outcomes. |
| Localization | Unsupported locale fallback, serialized preference changes, plural and formatting cases. | OS/app locale transitions, RTL layouts, long text, and native UI not owned by the shared layer. |
| Push | Payload validation, expiry, deduplication, session and destination decisions. | Permission denial, token refresh, foreground/background/cold start, and authorized navigation. |
| Branding | Registry membership, authorization inputs, coherent token/asset selection, failed preparation. | Launcher and alternate-icon behavior across supported versions and restart/upgrade paths. |
| Cleanup | Writer barrier, stage failures, idempotent retry, pending journal, and completion rules. | Real registered stores, keys, files, subscriptions, process interruption, and restart recovery. |

### Example: cleanup must not report false completion

**File: `src/core/wipe/cleanupCoordinator.test.ts`**

Use the project's Jest configuration. This suite exercises the TypeScript coordinator above through controlled ports; it does not delete real data.

```typescript
import { cleanAccount, type CleanupPorts } from './cleanupCoordinator';

function fixture() {
  const calls: string[] = [];
  const record = async (name: string): Promise<void> => { calls.push(name); };
  const ports: CleanupPorts = {
    blockProtectedAccess: () => { calls.push('block'); },
    recordPending: () => record('checkpoint'),
    stopWriters: () => record('stop-writers'),
    removeCredentials: () => record('credentials'),
    removeData: () => record('data'),
    removeCaches: () => record('caches'),
    destroyKeys: () => record('keys'),
    resetMemoryAndRedux: () => record('memory'),
    verify: () => record('verify'),
    markComplete: () => record('complete'),
    reportFailure: stage => { calls.push(`failure:${stage}`); },
  };
  return { ports, calls };
}

it('marks completion only after verification succeeds', async () => {
  const { ports, calls } = fixture();
  const result = await cleanAccount({ accountRef: 'synthetic-account' }, ports);
  expect(result).toEqual({ status: 'complete', failedStages: [] });
  expect(calls).toEqual([
    'block', 'checkpoint', 'stop-writers', 'credentials', 'data',
    'caches', 'keys', 'memory', 'verify', 'complete',
  ]);
});

it('does not delete resources when writers cannot be stopped', async () => {
  const { ports, calls } = fixture();
  ports.stopWriters = async () => { throw new Error('synthetic failure'); };
  const result = await cleanAccount({ accountRef: 'synthetic-account' }, ports);
  expect(result).toEqual({ status: 'incomplete', failedStages: ['stop-writers'] });
  expect(calls).toEqual(['block', 'checkpoint', 'failure:stop-writers']);
});

it('continues independent stages but preserves incomplete status', async () => {
  const { ports, calls } = fixture();
  ports.removeCredentials = async () => { throw new Error('synthetic failure'); };
  const result = await cleanAccount({ accountRef: 'synthetic-account' }, ports);
  expect(result).toEqual({ status: 'incomplete', failedStages: ['credentials'] });
  expect(calls).toEqual([
    'block', 'checkpoint', 'stop-writers', 'failure:credentials',
    'data', 'caches', 'keys', 'memory', 'verify',
  ]);
});
```

Also test checkpoint failure, verification failure, completion-journal failure, repeated execution, invalid scope rejection by the owner, conflicting login/cleanup, and restart from each pending stage. Translate the same behavioral assertions into Kotlin coroutine tests and Swift actor tests for their respective coordinators. Different cancellation contracts require platform-specific assertions rather than a mechanical syntax translation.

Testing & Quality owns the full framework setup, coverage enforcement, and Sauce Labs device matrix. CI/CD owns execution and gate integration.


